# nginx allocates three things once and lets the process exit take them
# back, so valgrind calls them lost.  None comes from this module: every
# trace ends in nginx's own code, and the module appears in none of them.
#
# These three are the only entries, and that is measured, not assumed.  With
# an empty file the suite reports exactly these and nothing else -- no
# invalid read, no uninitialised value, no conditional jump.  Anything in
# here beyond them would suppress something that never happens.
#
# The third one, the pool of the master cycle, is new in this repository and
# was not needed by the sibling modules.  Its trace is
# posix_memalign <- ngx_memalign <- ngx_create_pool <- main, which is nginx
# before a single module is loaded.
#
# Note which cycle function they come through: Test::Nginx runs nginx with
# master_process off, so everything here is the single-process path.  An
# entry for the master's own path would never be reached from this suite
# and so would be an entry nobody can check.
#
# If a later nginx brings a finding back, valgrind will say so with a full
# trace.  Add it then, with a name and a reason, rather than keeping one
# here on the chance that it is still needed.

{
   nginx keeps the environment array until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

{
   nginx keeps the connection and event arrays until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   ...
}
{
   nginx keeps the pool of the master cycle until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:posix_memalign
   fun:ngx_memalign
   fun:ngx_create_pool
   fun:main
}
